<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # So Many Different Historical Events to Learn! – Lesson # 4</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning to Research History</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> So Many Different Historical Events to Learn!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write short, simple essays with basic structure on familiar topics. Interactive: Provide feedback on peers' essays, including suggested edits.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a short essay summarizing a historical event and reflecting on its impact (e.g., "Panama separated from Colombia in 1903, which allowed the country to shape its national identity and control its own policies."). Can edit a peer's essay for clarity and organization (e.g., "Try starting with the most important event!"). Can provide feedback on a classmate's essay about Panama's history (e.g., "You might add more details about the date!").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of significant Panamanian historical events (e.g., the construction of the Panama Canal, Panama's independence declaration). Students engage in a quick "Think-Pair-Share" activity. Individually, they recall one fact they *learned* about each event. Then, in pairs, they share and discuss briefly, using past simple and phrases like "Historians say..." or "It was reported that...". This activates prior knowledge and sets the context for writing about historical *events* and their *significance*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write a simple factual sentence and then expand it with more detail and grammatical complexity relevant to historical reports. For example, "Panama *declared* independence in 1903." Then, expanding: "Panama *declared* independence in 1903, which *shaped* its national *identity*. Historians *reported* that this was a turning point for the country's *economy*." The teacher highlights the use of past simple and reported speech, and practices the pronunciation of '-ed' endings in 'declared' /d/ and 'shaped' /t/ and the stress in 'reported' (verb, second syllable). The teacher also clarifies that the goal is to *summarize* and *reflect* on impact.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we focusing on writing about future predictions or past *events*?" (Expected answer: Past events.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'summarize its *impact*', what does 'impact' mean?" (Expected answer: How it affected or changed things.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short historical report excerpt (approx. 150-200 words) about the *construction* and *legacy* of the Panama Canal. The text will explicitly use past simple, passive voice, reported speech, and target vocabulary (e.g., *canal*, *economy*, *engineering*, *marvel*, *transformation*, *legacy*, *completed*, *began*, *transferred*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will be able to *summarize* key information from a historical text, identifying the main *events* and their *significance* to inform their own writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main historical *event* described in the text?" "What did the author say about the canal's *impact* on Panama's *economy*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> Students read specific statements based on the text (e.g., "The canal was *completed* before 1900." "Historians *reported* that the canal only affected global trade, not Panama's *economy*.") and mark them as true or false.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match dates (e.g., 1903, 1914) with the corresponding *events* (e.g., *separation*, canal *completion*) mentioned in the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using past simple for historical *events*, passive voice for describing actions, and reported speech. They will actively use the target vocabulary: *history, Panama, independence, separation, canal, significance, report, construction, economy, diversity, events, culture, period, identity, legacy, heritage*, and verbs like *declared, shaped, reported, completed, began, transformed, compare, describe, summarize, reflect*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Sentence Transformation):</b> Students receive sentences about Panamanian *history*. They must rewrite them using the specified grammar.
                        <br><i>Example:</i> "Panama achieved independence in 1903." → Rewrite using "was declared": "Panama's *independence* *was declared* in 1903."
                        <br><i>Example:</i> "The president said, 'The canal transformed our *economy*.'" → Rewrite using reported speech: "The president *reported* that the canal *transformed* their *economy*."
                        <br>Students also fill-in-the-blanks with the correct past simple or passive form of target verbs (e.g., "The new *routes* ____ (create) a global *economy*." -> "created").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Practice - Guided Paragraph Writing):</b> Students are given a topic sentence (e.g., "The *separation* of Panama from Colombia was a defining *event*.") and a list of 3-4 keywords (e.g., *identity, shaped, significance, 1903*). In small groups, they write 2-3 additional sentences, incorporating the keywords and practicing past simple, passive voice, or reported speech. The teacher encourages them to *describe* the *events* and *reflect* on their *impact*. (e.g., "Panama's *separation* from Colombia in 1903 was a defining *event*. This action *shaped* the nation's *identity*, and its *significance* cannot be underestimated.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Collaborative Drafting & Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students choose one Panamanian historical *event* (e.g., the *construction* of the Panama Canal, the *transfer* of the Canal to Panama). They then collaboratively draft a short introductory paragraph (3-4 sentences) that *summarizes* the event and states its main *impact*. They exchange their paragraph with another pair and check for: 1) correct use of past simple and passive voice; 2) inclusion of at least three target vocabulary words; 3) clarity and organization. They use pragmatic language like, "You might add more details about the date!" or "Does this sentence clearly explain the *significance*?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Individual writing of a short essay (approx. 100-120 words) summarizing a historical event, followed by structured peer feedback and self-correction. Students will use the knowledge and skills *gained* from previous stages.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student chooses one important Panamanian historical *event* (e.g., *Panama's separation from Colombia in 1903*, *the construction of the Panama Canal*, *the transfer of the Canal to Panama*, or a significant *cultural* *period*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students independently write a short essay, structured into 3 paragraphs:
                        <br><b>Paragraph 1:</b> Introduce the *event* (when it *began*, what it *was*).
                        <br><b>Paragraph 2:</b> *Describe* the key details of the *event* (using past simple, passive voice).
                        <br><b>Paragraph 3:</b> *Reflect* on its *impact* or *legacy* on Panama's *identity*, *economy*, or *culture*. They should try to include reported speech (e.g., "Historians say...", "Many *reported* that...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering support and encouraging the use of the target vocabulary and grammatical structures. The teacher reminds students to consider the pronunciation of '-ed' endings and stress in multi-syllable words like 'research' (noun/verb) as they review their own writing silently.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Peer assessment using a checklist focusing on writing criteria, followed by teacher observation of feedback interactions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their essays with a partner. Using a provided checklist, they read their peer's essay and provide written feedback, including suggested edits. This allows them to practice the interactive part of the learning outcome ("Can edit a peer's essay for clarity and organization" and "Can provide feedback on a classmate's essay").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is there a clear introduction to the historical *event*?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are past simple verbs and passive voice used accurately to *describe* the *events*?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is there an attempt to use reported speech?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., *independence, separation, canal, economy, identity, legacy*) used appropriately?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Does the essay clearly *reflect* on the *impact* or *significance* of the *event*?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are there any suggestions for improving clarity or *organization* (e.g., "Try starting with the most important event!")?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is the feedback provided helpful and constructive?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confidently can I write a short essay *summarizing* a historical *event* and reflecting on its *impact*?" (Scale of 1-5)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing my essay or giving *feedback* to my peer?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new vocabulary or grammar structures did I *gain* confidence in using today while writing about *history*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did giving and receiving *feedback* on the essays help me to *understand* better how to *summarize* historical *events* and *reflect* on their *legacy*?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to foster students' writing skills? (e.g., "The sentence transformation practice effectively prepared students for using passive voice.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time, especially regarding essay structure or integration of reported speech? (e.g., "Some students struggled with *organization* and integrating reported speech naturally. I need to provide more explicit sentence frames for reported speech.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the pronunciation practice for '-ed' endings and stress help students in their self-correction during writing?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their revised historical essays to create a visual timeline or a digital presentation. This will involve mediating the information for a different audience, summarizing their written work, and integrating their understanding of historical *events* into a collaborative 21st-century skills project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>